## Where, Why, How

**Where.** The `capture_inverse` implementations on `UpdateCard`, `UpdateColumn`, and `UpdateSprint` all repeat the same field-by-field inversion pattern for `FieldUpdate<T>`-typed optional fields:

- `crates/kanban-domain/src/commands/card_commands.rs:136-184` (`UpdateCard::capture_inverse`)
- `crates/kanban-domain/src/commands/column_commands.rs:66-90` (`UpdateColumn::capture_inverse`)
- `crates/kanban-domain/src/commands/sprint_commands.rs:97-160` (`UpdateSprint::capture_inverse`)

**Why.** The pattern repeats ~10 times across the three files. Each occurrence is a four-line match that's mechanical noise:

```rust
description: match upd.description {
    FieldUpdate::NoChange => FieldUpdate::NoChange,
    _ => match card.description {
        Some(v) => FieldUpdate::Set(v),
        None => FieldUpdate::Clear,
    },
},
```

Every reader has to parse "what does this do?" again. A named helper says it once: `invert_optional(&upd.description, &card.description)` reads as "give me the inverse of this optional-field patch." The behaviour is identical; only the names of the field on `upd` and on the entity change between sites.

Confirmed by two independent DRY audits (handler-DRY + domain-command DRY).

**How.** Add one helper in `kanban-domain/src/field_update.rs` next to `FieldUpdate<T>` itself. Replace every 4-line match with a one-line call. Zero behaviour change, all tests pass unchanged.

