## Context / why
The SQLite schema **2 -> 3** upgrade (KAN-832 / B4) runs automatically on `SqliteStore::open()` and is **irreversible**: an older binary rejects `schema_version = 3` (`mod.rs:91` `if v > SUPPORTED_SCHEMA_VERSION { return Err(UnsupportedFutureVersion ...) }`). A user who installs a build with this migration, opens their DB (silently upgrading it), then reverts to an older build can no longer open their data. Unlike the JSON backend there is **no file-level backup at all** on the SQLite side.

Nuance discovered while filing this (so the card is accurate):
- The migration itself is wrapped in `BEGIN ... COMMIT` (see `init.rs migrate_v2_to_v3_archived_cards`), so a **mid-migration crash is already safe** (it rolls back to schema 2). This card is **not** about crash-safety.
- The JSON `.v7.backup` is a *transient crash-safety* copy that is **removed on successful migration** (`test_load_sync_vN_to_v7_cleans_up_vN_backup_on_success`), so JSON also leaves no durable downgrade copy on success.
- The real gap is a **durable, kept-on-success pre-upgrade snapshot** so a user can roll back an irreversible format bump. SQLite is the worse case because it has zero file backup.

The migration doc comment currently claims **"SQLite has no backup mechanism"** (`crates/kanban-persistence-sqlite/src/sqlite_store/init.rs:150`). That is **inaccurate** — `VACUUM INTO` (SQLite >= 3.27) writes a transactionally-consistent single-file copy. This card corrects that comment.

