## Code examples
Enum + dispatch (before→after, `board_commands.rs:20-40`):
```rust
// BEFORE (enum tail)         // AFTER
    Import(ImportEntities),       Import(ImportEntities),
    RestoreSprintPool(...),       RestoreSprintPool(...),
}                                 ArchiveBoard(ArchiveBoard),
                                  RestoreBoard(RestoreBoard),
                              }
// execute match — add:
    BoardCommand::ArchiveBoard(c) => c.execute(context),
    BoardCommand::RestoreBoard(c) => c.execute(context),
// (same for description and capture_inverse)
```
Symmetric-inverse rationale: `capture_inverse` runs *before* `execute` (undo.rs), but the inverse command runs *after* the forward during undo. So `ArchiveBoard`'s inverse `RestoreBoard` executes on an already-archived board (clears the marker); `RestoreBoard`'s inverse `ArchiveBoard` executes on an already-live board (sets it). Neither needs a captured aggregate — the state to restore is never destroyed, only marked. This is strictly simpler than the `RestoreCard`⇄`ArchiveCards` pair (which must move a card), because a board archive moves nothing.

